{
  "python_code": "async def act(page):\n    \"\"\"\n    Perform a site search for the keyword 'GPT' using the site's visible complementary\n    search widget (via the knowledge-base helper), then click the third search result.\n\n    Notes:\n    - Uses create_curated_collection_by_search(...) from the knowledge base to interact\n      with the visible search UI. That helper begins by navigating to '/', finds the\n      complementary region, fills the search input, triggers the search, and waits for\n      results to appear under role='main'.\n    - After the helper returns, locate the third role='article' inside role='main' and\n      click the link inside its level=2 heading. All .nth() usages are guarded by\n      a prior await .count() to avoid indexing errors.\n    \"\"\"\n\n    # Use the KB helper to perform the visible search for 'GPT'.\n    # Utility of KB functions considered:\n    # - create_curated_collection_by_search: This is the appropriate helper for using the\n    #   site's visible complementary-region search widget; it handles navigation to '/',\n    #   locating the complementary region and search control, activating the search, and\n    #   waiting for results in role='main'. We use it to reliably perform the search.\n    # - create_curated_collection_and_newsletter: Not needed for this simple search-and-click task\n    #   (it wraps the search helper and also generates a newsletter), so we do not call it.\n    await create_curated_collection_by_search(page, \"GPT\")\n\n    # After the search, results are expected inside role='main' as role='article' children.\n    main = page.get_by_role(\"main\")\n    articles = main.get_by_role(\"article\")\n\n    # Guard .nth() with a count() call to avoid indexing errors.\n    count = await articles.count()\n    if count < 3:\n        raise Exception(f\"Expected at least 3 search results for 'GPT', but found {count}.\")\n\n    # Select the third article (index 2) and click the title link inside its level=2 heading.\n    third_article = articles.nth(2)\n    title_link = third_article.get_by_role(\"heading\", level=2).get_by_role(\"link\")\n\n    # Click the link (may navigate away) as the final action.\n    await title_link.click()\n",
