 third article's title link.\n    main = page.get_by_role(\"main\")\n    articles = main.get_by_role(\"article\")\n\n    # Precede .nth() with a count check to avoid indexing errors.\n    count = await articles.count()\n    if count < 3:\n        raise Exception(f\"Expected at least 3 results, but found {count}.\")\n\n    third_article = articles.nth(2)\n    # The article's title is a heading (level=2) that contains a link. Use the full path from main->article->heading->link.\n    title_link = third_article.get_by_role(\"heading\", level=2).get_by_role(\"link\")\n\n    # Click the title link. This is expected to navigate away, so do this as the final action.\n    await title_link.click()\n\n</code>\n\n<result>\n10\n</result>\n\n---\n\n<reasoning>\n1) The task is to use the site's search widget to search for 'GPT' and click the third result. The knowledge-base exposes create_curated_collection_by_search(...), which is explicitly designed to use the visible complementary search widget and produce results accessible via role='main' and role='article'. The instructions require using such KB helpers when available for the corresponding action, so I will call create_curated_collection_by_search(page, \"GPT\").\n\n2) That helper's first action is to navigate to '/', which is acceptable; it locates the complementary region, fills the search input, triggers the search, and waits for results to appear in role='main'. Using it avoids re-implementing fragile accessibility-centric interactions.\n\n3) After the helper returns, I locate search results under main.get_by_role('article'). Before indexing with .nth(2) I call await articles.count() to ensure there are at least three results, avoiding indexing errors.\n\n4) I then use the full chained accessibility-role path from main->article->heading(level=2)->link to identify the article's title link unambiguously and click it. Clicking the link may navigate away, so it is the last action.\n\n5) If fewer than three results are found 